ial; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for publish. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with publish, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Publishing a neat one-sided rant still fails discussion purpose.. Correction: Move paragraph = revise;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w30-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise publish.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use publish with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify discussion and explain why it matters.","I can practise revise independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Publishing a discussion text means revising for balance and clarity, editing surface errors and presenting it for a real audience.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Publishing a discussion text means revising for balance and clarity, editing surface errors and presenting it for a","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about publish with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRevision: add a missing against point. Edit: capitals and contrast commas. Publish with a title that names the issue, not \"My Work\".\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort revise vs edit: move against paragraph earlier; fix spelling of however.\n\nFEEDBACK\nMove paragraph = revise; spelling = edit.","explain":"Publishing a discussion text means revising for balance and clarity, editing surface errors and presenting it for a real audience.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Publishing a neat one-sided rant still fails discussion purpose. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Publish path: revise then edit.","caption":"Title names the issue.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Revise</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">add missing against</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">capitals + commas</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Revision: add a missing against point. Edit: capitals and contrast commas. Publish with a title that names the issue, no Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Revision: add a missing against point. Edit: capitals and contrast commas. Publish with a title that names the issue, no Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about publish with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRevision: add a missing against point. Edit: capitals and contrast commas. Publish with a title that names the issue, not \"My Work\". Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Publication with reflection vs neat rant.","caption":"Serve discussion purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Author note</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one revision named</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">neat one-sided rant</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about publish with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPublishing a neat one-sided rant still fails discussion purpose. Here is a more productive model: Revision: add a missing against point. Edit: capitals and contrast commas. Publish with a title that names the issue, not \"My Work\".\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nMove paragraph = revise; spelling = edit.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Publishing a neat one-sided rant still fails discussion purpose. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about publish with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPublishing a discussion text means revising for balance and clarity, editing surface errors and presenting it for a real audience. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 30, Lesson 2: Publishing a discussion text","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Discussion texts and comparing viewpoints","week":30,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nRevision: add a missing against point. Edit: capitals and contrast commas. Publish with a title that names the issue, not \"My Work\".\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Publish path: revise then edit.","caption":"Title names the issue.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Revise</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">add missing against</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">capitals + commas</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Publication with reflection vs neat rant.","caption":"Serve discussion purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Author note</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one revision named</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">neat one-sided ra